<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/intermediate/speaking-and-shadowing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Speaking is the skill learners avoid most and need most. It is also the skill that
    behaves most differently from the others: reading and listening can hide gaps;
    production <strong>forces retrieval</strong>, exposes every gap in real time, and
    trains the mouth's motor patterns. This lesson is the output strategy — shadowing for
    solo daily practice, tutors and exchanges for real conversation, and the mindset that
    treats errors as data. The A2 ladder states the target plainly: "can communicate in
    simple and routine tasks", "can describe in simple terms aspects of his/her
    background, immediate environment and matters in areas of immediate need" — the
    can-dos are all production.
  </p>
  <p>
    One framing resolves the input/output argument before it starts: input builds the
    understanding; output builds the machine that uses it. Learners who only input
    understand much and produce haltingly; learners who only output fossilise confidently.
    The ratio shifts over time (input-heavy early, output-heavy later), but the speaking
    hours are non-negotiable from the start — lightly.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="output hypothesis, shadowing, conversation sources, error mindset" widget="equationStep" config={speakCfg} />

  <h2>Why output is non-negotiable: the evidence</h2>
  <p>
    The founding evidence is Merrill Swain's work on Canadian French immersion (1985):
    children with thousands of classroom input hours understood nearly everything and
    still spoke with persistent grammatical imprecision — comprehension had outpaced
    production because production demands were low. Output, she argued, does three things
    input cannot: it <strong>notices the gap</strong> (you discover you have no way to say
    what you mean), it <strong>forces syntactic processing</strong> (comprehension can
    skate on semantics; production must choose forms), and it generates
    <strong>feedback</strong>. Modern formulations add the memory science: speaking is
    retrieval practice, and retrieval at the edge of ability is what strengthens both
    vocabulary and grammar far more than recognition ever does. "I understand it but I
    can't say it" is not a character flaw — it is the predictable result of never having
    been made to say it.
  </p>

  <h2>Shadowing: the daily fluency gym</h2>
  <p>
    Shadowing (the technique associated with Alexander Arguelles) is speaking along with
    native audio in real time, matching rhythm, stress and speed — not waiting for gaps,
    but overlaying. The protocol that works: pick a 60–90 second clip of clear speech;
    listen once for meaning; then shadow it 5–10 times, first just riding the melody,
    then articulating fully; move to a new clip when it flows. Difficulty is adjustable —
    slower learner audio early, native podcasts later — and the benefits stack the three
    production bottlenecks into one exercise: articulation (mouth muscles under time
    pressure), prosody (the melody layer the pronunciation lesson flagged as what
    listeners actually hear), and speed (real-time processing without translation pauses).
    Ten to fifteen minutes daily is a complete programme; the measurable signature is
    that sentences you shadowed last week come out of your mouth whole this week.
  </p>

  <h2>The conversation diet: tutors, exchanges, meetups</h2>
  <p>
    Real conversation needs humans, and the market has solved access: online tutors
    (italki, Preply and similar — inexpensive, schedule-flexible, ideal for structured
    speaking practice), language exchanges (you trade your language for theirs — free,
    social, variable in quality), and conversation meetups. The scarce resource in all
    three is <strong>your talking time</strong>, so manage it explicitly: an hour where
    you speak forty minutes beats three hours where you speak ten. Tactics that raise the
    ratio — book conversation-focused tutors and say so up front; in exchanges, agree
    timing splits (ten minutes each, alternating); have topics prepared (ten phrases for
    the topic, then improvise) so the session never dies in logistics; and give tutors a
    standing instruction: note my recurring errors, review the list in the last five
    minutes, do not interrupt for anything else.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last instruction encodes a real distinction: <strong>fluency sessions and
    accuracy sessions are different workouts</strong>. Fluency sessions (free talk,
    description games, no interruptions) build speed, courage and automaticity. Accuracy
    work (error review, targeted drills on the log, rehearsal of yesterday's failures)
    builds precision. Mixing them produces neither — the classic session where the tutor
    corrects every third sentence trains you to speak less, which is the opposite of the
    goal.
  </p>

  <h2>Speaking before you're ready</h2>
  <p>
    The psychological layer decides who actually accumulates speaking hours.
    Perfectionism delays fluency by construction: the learner who waits until ready is
    scheduling production after the input has already plateaued. The reframes that work:
    errors with feedback are the fastest teachers (the grammar lesson's data, not sins);
    your listener's patience is nearly always higher than you model it (people help
    willing speakers); and <strong>willingness to communicate</strong> — the research
    construct (MacIntyre and colleagues) predicting who uses a language given the chance
    — is itself trainable by repeated low-stakes attempts. The free supplements:
    <strong>self-talk</strong> (narrate your day internally in the target language —
    zero-cost output volume, available everywhere), prepared <strong>fillers</strong> that
    buy real-time planning space in a native-sounding way ("bueno, entonces…", "n'était-ce
    pas que…"), and the monthly <strong>two-minute monologue archive</strong>: record
    yourself on the same topic each month. The archive shows progress the daily
    experience hides — the single best antidote to plateau despair.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> more input will eventually make speaking automatic.
  It will not — the conversion from understanding to producing is a distinct stage of
  skill, and it is trained only by producing. Input first and mostly, yes; input only,
  never.</p>

  <h2>A worked shadowing month</h2>
  <p>
    The technique at programme scale. Week 1 — pick five clips of 60–90 seconds from
    one speaker (consistency helps early; a clear news presenter or a vlogger with
    unhurried speech). Shadow each clip five times on its day, priority on melody over
    articulation. Record day 1 and day 7 doing the same clip. Week 2 — same five clips,
    full articulation, plus a sixth clip per day from new material; compare the weekly
    re-recording. Week 3 — move to 2–3 minute clips and one "cold shadow" session daily
    (first listen is also the first shadow — pure real-time processing). Week 4 —
    switch speakers entirely; the melody habits should transfer. Month-end test:
    record a two-minute monologue on a set topic and compare with week 1's — the
    expected differences are pause frequency (down), pace (up), and the disappearance
    of the translation pause before verbs. If nothing moved, the clips were too hard;
    drop a difficulty notch and run the month again. Shadowing is measurable, which
    is what makes it trainable.
  </p>

  <h2>The scaffolding phrase set: fluency from thirty sentences</h2>
  <p>
    Fluent-sounding conversation at A2 is mostly <strong>conversation management</strong>,
    and that is a finite, learnable phrase set: clarification ("sorry, slower please",
    "what does X mean?"), buying time ("well, let me think…"), agreeing and
    redirecting ("that's true, but…"), repairing ("how do you say… the thing for
    opening bottles?"), and courtesy frames that keep exchanges alive. Thirty such
    sentences, drilled to automaticity, change the experience of conversation from
    cliff-edge to walkway — they buy the seconds in which your real grammar can load.
    The deeper skill behind one of them, <strong>circumlocution</strong>, deserves
    deliberate practice: describing around a missing word ("the thing for…", "it's
    like a… but bigger") instead of freezing or switching to English. Native speakers
    do it constantly in their own language; learners who train it become
    conversation-proof, because no vocabulary gap can stop them for long. Drill it as
    a game: pick a random object, describe it without naming it, thirty seconds.
  </p>

  <h2>The tutor session, engineered</h2>
  <p>
    Sixty paid minutes have an architecture. Minutes 0–10: warm-up free talk (yesterday,
    plans, weather — fluency mode, no corrections). Minutes 10–35: the prepared topic
    (this week's theme from the grammar lesson's loop — past narration, say — with your
    ten prepared phrases deployed and improvised around; the tutor asks follow-ups
    rather than correcting). Minutes 35–50: error review (the tutor reads back the
    notes from the free talk and the topic; you re-form each error aloud — production
    of the correction, not just hearing it; this is where accuracy work actually
    happens). Minutes 50–60: set next week's topic and homework (a monologue to
    record, a pattern to shadow). Two standing instructions make the hour yours: "maximise
    my talking time" and "note, don't interrupt". Learners who run this structure
    consistently report the same discovery: the tutor's value is not the explanation —
    it is the engineered scarcity of your own silence.
  </p>

  <h2>The topic bank: never start from silence</h2>
  <p>
    The practical enemy of speaking volume is the empty minute — conversation dies and
    the learner retreats. A <strong>topic bank</strong> solves it: twenty subjects you
    can genuinely talk about (your work, your city, a hobby, a film, a plan), each with
    ten prepared phrases and five likely questions you might be asked. Rotate one topic
    per tutoring session and one per self-talk walk; refresh the bank monthly with
    material harvested from your input (a podcast episode becomes a topic; so does
    anything you read). The bank does three jobs at once: it guarantees material (no
    dead air), it recycles high-frequency chunks into spontaneous use (the grammar
    lesson's loop, weaponised), and it converts nervousness into preparation — the
    difference between improvising under threat and improvising over notes. Experienced
    learners run the same bank for years, deepening it; the topics mature with the
    language, and the starting line is never silence.
  </p>

  <h2>Repair: the skill under the skill</h2>
  <p>
    Real conversation is full of trouble — mishearings, missing words, botched endings —
    and native speakers navigate it with a repair repertoire so automatic it is
    invisible. Learners need it explicitly. The moves: <strong>request repetition</strong>
    politely and specifically ("sorry, the last part?"), <strong>confirm understanding</strong>
    ("so you're saying that…?"), <strong>signal a problem without switching languages</strong>
    ("how do I say…", plus circumlocution), and <strong>restart cleanly</strong> after a
    failed sentence rather than abandoning it. Practise them as drills — roleplay a
    mishearing with a tutor, run a whole conversation where you deliberately ask for
    clarification three times — because in the moment of trouble you will fall back on
    whatever is automatic, and "sorry, my X is bad" (the apologetic retreat, in your own
    language) is what most learners have made automatic instead. Learners who own repair
    keep talking through breakdowns, and every breakdown becomes input and feedback
    rather than a conversation-ending event. Fluency, it turns out, is not the absence
    of trouble — it is the quality of the recovery.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Fluency itself can be decomposed, and the decomposition turns practice into
    engineering. Researchers (following Skehan) measure it as three behaviours:
    <strong>speed</strong> (syllables per minute), <strong>breakdown</strong> (pauses —
    their frequency and length), and <strong>repair</strong> (restarts, corrections,
    false starts). Record a two-minute monologue and count: where are the pauses
    clustering — before verbs? before nouns you lack? at clause joins? The clusters
    tell you what to drill: pause-before-verb is grammar automation (shadowing and
    chunk practice fix it); pause-before-noun is vocabulary (the 1,000-words engine);
    restarts are planning (self-talk and prepared topic frames fix it). Fluency work
    at this level is just measurement plus targeted practice — the same loop the
    pronunciation lesson ran on sounds, run on time. The last expert note: automaticity
    is built from <em>chunks</em>, not from speeded-up word assembly — which is why the
    grammar lesson's chunk-first strategy and this lesson's shadowing are secretly the
    same bet: fluent speech comes from a large library of prefabricated pieces that
    production assembles under pressure, and every method here is a way of stocking
    that library.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Swain, "Communicative Competence: Some Roles of Comprehensible Input and Output" (1985); MacIntyre et al. on willingness to communicate; Skehan on fluency measurement; Arguelles on shadowing.</p>
